      "level": 1,
      "content": "Identity Matrix"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "An identity matrix is a square matrix where all the elements on the main diagonal (from the top left to the bottom right) are ones, and all the other elements are zeros."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Identity Matrix in NumPy",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "We can see the matrix has ones on the main diagonal and zeros elsewhere. This is the 3x3 identity matrix."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In NumPy, we use the np.eye() function or the np.identity() function to create an identity matrix."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "1import numpy as np\n2\n3# create a 2x2 identity matrix using eye()\n4matrix1 = np.eye(2)\n5\n6print(\"\\n2x2 Identity Matrix:\")\n7print(matrix1)\n8\n9# create a 3x3 identity matrix using identity()\n10matrix2 = np.identity(3)\n11\n12print(\"3x3 Identity Matrix:\")\n13print(matrix2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "2x2 Identity Matrix:\n[[1. 0.]\n [0. 1.]]\n3x3 Identity Matrix:\n[[1. 0. 0.]\n [0. 1. 0.]\n [0. 0. 1.]]"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Both np.eye() and np.identity() functions in NumPy return a matrix of floating-point numbers by default."
    },
